# Agent model for machines running models.conf (under 48GB of RAM).
# The 48GB+ equivalent is agent-big.Modelfile, FROM devstral:24b — only the
# one whose FROM base is actually present gets built.
#
# Same weights as chat, MUCH bigger context window.
#
#   ollama create agent -f modelfiles/agent.Modelfile
#   (setup-local-ai.sh builds this automatically if the base is present)
#
# ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ollama defaults num_ctx to roughly 2-4K tokens. An agent blows past that
# within a few tool calls — system prompt + file contents + diffs + command
# output add up fast. When it overflows, Ollama does NOT error: it SILENTLY
# TRUNCATES. The agent then forgets its own plan, re-reads files it just
# read, and loops. It looks like a stupid model. It has amnesia.
#
# This is the single most common way a local agent setup fails.
#
# ── THE COST: KV cache is RAM ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Context is not free. For a 14B at q8_0 the KV cache runs roughly
# ~100KB/token, on top of the weights:
#
#     16K ctx  ~= 1.6GB    <- safe starting point
#     32K ctx  ~= 3.2GB    <- fits on 24GB, but evict other models
#     64K ctx  ~= 6.4GB    <- needs a raised GPU cap
#
# Check reality rather than trusting the arithmetic:
#     ./scripts/model.sh free && ./scripts/ram.sh
#
# 16K is the recommended floor for agent work. Start there, raise if needed.

FROM qwen2.5-coder:14b

PARAMETER num_ctx 16384
PARAMETER temperature 0.1
PARAMETER top_p 0.9

# Agents need determinism more than flair — they follow a plan rather than
# writing prose, and low temperature also reduces malformed tool calls.

SYSTEM """
You are a coding agent operating on a real repository.

Read before you write. Prefer the smallest change that solves the problem.
Follow the existing style of the file you are editing.
State assumptions explicitly. If you are unsure, say so rather than inventing
an API or a file path.
After editing, verify: run the test or the command that proves it works.
"""
